## Part A — Creating Relations Between Entities

## **A.1 — What Is an Entity Relation?**

An Entity Relation tells the Klobase server how two entities are connected to each other. Without a relation defined in the BOL Editor, the platform has no way to automatically load child records when a parent record is selected — and no way to bundle the BO for save/delete operations.

There are two types of relations in Klobase:

| Relation Type | What It Means |
| :---- | :---- |
| Primary Relation | Tight parent-child bond WITHIN the same Business Object (BO). A child entity is dependent on the parent. Prefixed pr\_. Used for d\_complaint\_item, d\_complaint\_voices, d\_complaints\_attachment — all children of d\_complaints. |
| Non-Primary (Aggregated) Relation | Looser link BETWEEN BOs. Used to reference standalone master entities. Prefixed r\_. Used for d\_customers and d\_resp\_team which are independent master entities. |

| 📘  Why Relations Matter in the UI |
| :---- |
| When a relation is defined and referenced in a TransNode in the Page Builder, the platform automatically fetches the child/linked entity records when the parent record is loaded. |
| Without a relation, you would have to write manual queries and wire up the data fetch yourself. |
| The TransNode type 'Relation' in the Page Data Model uses the relation name defined here — this is why naming the relation correctly matters. |
| Naming convention (from BOL Editor 7.0 User Guide — Best Practices): pr\_ prefix for primary relations, r\_ prefix for associated/aggregated relations. Always lowercase. |
|  |

## **A.2 — Relations to Create for d\_complaints**

You will create 5 relations on the d\_complaints root entity — one for each related entity.

| Relation ID | Type | Return Entity | Cardinality |
| :---- | :---- | :---- | :---- |
| r\_complaint\_item | Non-Primary (Aggregated) | d\_complaint\_item | 1:n (One complaint → Many items) |
| r\_complaint\_voices | Non-Primary (Aggregated) | d\_complaint\_voices | 1:n (One complaint → Many voices) |
| r\_complaint\_attach | Non-Primary (Aggregated) | d\_complaints\_attachment | 1:n (One complaint → Many attachments) |
| r\_customer | Non-Primary (Aggregated) | d\_customers | 1:1 (One complaint has one customer) |
| r\_resp\_team | Non-Primary (Aggregated) | d\_resp\_team | 1:1 (One complaint has one responsible team member) |

## **A.3 — Step-by-Step: Creating a Primary Relation**

Primary Relation will be automatically  created with the creation of child entity and requires no manual action

## **A.4 — Step-by-Step: Creating a Non-Primary (Aggregated) Relation**

Non-primary relations link d\_complaints to standalone master entities (d\_customers and d\_resp\_team). Follow these steps for r\_customer

| \# | Action | Detail |
| ----- | :---- | :---- |
| **1** | **d\_complaints Relations tab** | click d\_complaints in the tree and go back to the Relations tab. |
| **2** | **Click Add** | Click the Add (+) icon to add a new relation row. |
| **3** | **Fill Relation Details** | Relation Name: r\_customer | Description: Aggregated relation — customer master data | Return Entity: d\_customers | Relation Query: d\_customers | Cardinality: 1:1 |
| **4** | **Configure Mapping** | Click Mapping. Add: Source Property \= customer\_id | Mapping Type \= ON QUERY | Target Property \= customer\_id. This join fetches d\_customers data when a complaint is loaded. |
| **5** | **Save** | Click Save. |

| 💡  Mapping Type — BOTH Explained |
| :---- |
| ON CREATE: When a new d\_complaint\_item is created under a d\_complaints parent, the platform automatically copies the complaint\_id value into the ticket\_id field of the new item. You never have to set this manually. |
| ON QUERY: When you query d\_complaint\_item records for a given complaint, the platform uses ticket\_id \= complaint\_id as the WHERE condition automatically. |
| BOTH: Use this for standard parent-child relations — it handles both the create and the fetch scenarios. |
|  |

Now repeat the same steps to create the remaining relations:

| Relation ID | Return Entity | Mapping (Source → Target) |
| :---- | :---- | :---- |
| r\_complaint\_voices | d\_complaint\_voices | complaint\_id → ticket\_id | Cardinality: 1:n | Mapping Type: BOTH |
| r\_complaint\_items | d\_complaint\_item | complaint\_id → ticket\_id| Cardinality: 1:n | Mapping Type: BOTH |
| r\_complaint\_attach | d\_complaints\_attachment | complaint\_id → complaint\_id| Cardinality: 1:n | Mapping Type: BOTH |
| r\_resp\_team | d\_resp\_team | responsible\_team → emp\_id | Cardinality: 1:1 | Mapping Type: ON QUERY |

## Part B — Configuring Object Types for d\_complaints

## **B.1 — What Is an Object Type?**

In Klobase, an Object Type (stored in the system property `s_object_type`) is a classification that tags each record as belonging to a specific type. Object Types serve two important purposes:

* They enable role-based visibility: a button or field can be made visible/editable only for a specific role **AND** a specific object type combination. This is used for screen behavior in Session 7\.  
* They allow ID Series to be object-type-specific: a different number series prefix can be applied per object type — for example, complaints created by Maventic employees might use a different prefix than those created by Procify employees.

| 📘  Object Type vs Object Group |
| :---- |
| **Object Group** is a container that groups related Object Types together. The group is used as the filter boundary. **Object Type** is the actual tag applied to individual records (e.g. MAVENTIC, PROCIFY). Every Object Type must belong to exactly one Object Group. For the Customer Complaints application: Object Group \= `company_types` Object Types \= `MAVENTIC`, `PROCIFY` **Best Practice (BOL Editor 7.0 User Guide):** Always maintain an Object Group to group types — the same group can be used in default filters based on users. |
|  |

## **B.2 — Object Types for d\_complaints**

| Object Type ID | Description |
| :---- | :---- |
| MAVENTIC | Complaints created by Maventic employees |
| PROCIFY | Complaints created by Procify employees |

## **B.3 — Step-by-Step: Creating Object Group and Object Types**

| \# | Action | Detail |
| ----- | :---- | :---- |
| **1** | **Go to Object Type tab** | Select d\_complaints in the tree. Click the Object Type tab in the Detail panel. The tab shows two sections: the upper section for Object Types  |
| **2** | **Open Object Group dialog** | In the Object Type section, click the dialog icon next to the Object Group field to open the Object Group management dialog. |
| **3** | **Create Object Group** | In the Object Group dialog, click Add. Fill in: Object Group ID: complaint\_types | Description: Complaint type classifications. Click Save inside the dialog. |
| **4** | **Close dialog** | Click Cancel / Close on the Object Group dialog to return to the Object Type tab. |
| **5** | **Add first Object Type** | Fill: Object Type: `MAVENTIC` | Description : `MAVENTIC` | Object Group: `complaint_types` (select from value help). Click Save. |
| **6** | **Add remaining types** | Repeat Add for: `PROCIFY` — under Object Group: `complaint_types`. Click Save after all are added. |
| **7** | **Verify** | All Object Types appear in the list, all with Object Group: complaint\_types. |

## 

After adding the  object type to differentiate complaints based on company is only half in order to achieve difference in complaint\_id based on company another part is pending which is configuring ID Series based on object\_type. 

## Part C — Setting Up ID Series for Auto-Numbering

## **D.1 — What Is an ID Series?**

An ID Series is a platform-managed counter that automatically generates primary key values when a new record is created. Without an ID Series on complaint\_id, a developer would need to generate unique IDs manually — which is error-prone and cannot produce readable, formatted numbers like CMP-00001.

| ID Series Type | Description |
| :---- | :---- |
| Auto Number | Returns an incrementing number. Combined with a Prefix pattern to produce CMP-00001, CMP-00002, etc. |
| GUID | Returns a 32-character UUID. Good for distributed offline scenarios. Not human-readable. |
| None | No auto-generation. The developer or rule must set the key manually. |

## **D.2 — ID Series to Configure**

| Property | Type | Company | Pattern/Prefix | Start From |
| :---- | :---- | :---- | :---- | :---- |
| complaint\_id | Auto Number | PROCIFY | Prefix: P- (generates P-00001) | 1 |
| complaint\_id | Auto Number | MAVENTIC | Prefix: M \- (generates M-00001) | 1 |

## **D.3 — Step-by-Step: Creating the ID Series**

| \# | Action | Detail |
| ----- | :---- | :---- |
| **1** | **Select d\_complaints** | In the Data Studio tree, click on d\_complaints. |
| **2** | **Go to Properties tab** | Click the Properties tab in the Detail panel. Locate the complaint\_id property row. |
| **3** | **Open ID Series** | Click the navigation right arrow (→) at the end of the complaint\_id row. The ID Series dialog opens for this property. |
| **4** | **Click Add** | Click the Add (+) icon in the ID Series dialog. A new entry row appears. |
| **5** | **Fill ID Series details** | ID: idp\_complaint | Type: Auto Number | From: 1 | Prefix: P- | Chunk Size: 100 | Increment: 1 | Scope: Global | Description: Auto-number for complaint IDs with P- prefix. |
| **6** | **Click Add** | Click the Add (+) icon in the ID Series dialog. A new entry row appears. |
| **7** | **Fill ID Series details** | ID: idm\_complaint | Type: Auto Number | From: 1 | Prefix: M- | Chunk Size: 100 | Increment: 1 | Scope: Global | Description: Auto-number for complaint IDs with P- prefix. |
| **8** | **Close dialog** | Click Close on the dialog. The ID Series entry is saved. |
| **9** | **Save** | Click Save in the Data Studio toolbar. The complaint\_id property now has an ID Series configured. |

| 📘  ID Series Fields Reference (BOL Editor 7.0 User Guide — Section 3.0.1 Creating ID Series) |
| :---- |
| ID: Unique ID of the series. Naming convention: id\_ prefix, all lowercase. Example: id\_complaint. |
| Type: Auto Number \= incremental counter. Most common for business records. |
| From: Starting number for the series. Use 1 to start from P-00001. |
| Prefix: Text prepended before the number. P- produces P-1, P-2, etc. |
| Chunk Size: Default 100\. The server pre-allocates this many IDs per session (important for offline apps). |
| Scope: Global (default) — one counter shared across all users and tenants. |
| Pattern: Can be used to produce complex formats. For simple prefix \+ number, just use the Prefix field. |
|  |

## **D.4 — Testing the ID Series** {#d.4-—-testing-the-id-series}

| \# | Action | Detail |
| ----- | :---- | :---- |
| **1** | **Open Data Browser** | With d\_complaints selected, click Test in the toolbar. The Data Browser opens. |
| **2** | **Create a new record** | Click Add (+ Execute → Add → select object\_type). Leave complaint\_id blank — the platform will fill it. |
| **3** | **Fill minimal fields** | complaint\_desc: Test auto-number | priority: Low. Click Save. |
| **4** | **Verify** | The complaint\_id column should show P-00001 for Procify and M-0001 for Maventic (or the next available number). If it shows a blank or an error, revisit the ID Series configuration. |

## Part C — Configuring Status and Status Groups

## **C.1 — What Is Status in Klobase?**

The system property s\_status represents the current state of a complaint record. The Status tab in the Data Studio lets you define the allowed values for s\_status and group them into Status Groups.

Status Groups are named collections of statuses. They are used in Vienable (Session 7\) to control screen behaviour: for example, the Edit button is visible only when s\_status is in the OPEN\_GROUP, and the Assign Team button is visible only when s\_status is in the INPROCESS\_GROUP.

| 📘  Status Lifecycle for Customer Complaints |
| :---- |
| The complaint lifecycle defined in the BRD has 6 states: |
| OPEN → INPROCESS → ON\_HOLD / RESOLVED → CLOSED / REOPEN |
| Each status transition is triggered by a specific role (defined in the Role Responsibility matrix from Session 1 Part A). |
| In this session you define the status values and groups. The actual UI enforcement of transitions using Vienable happens in Session 7\. |
|  |

## **C.2 — Status Values and Groups to Configure**

| Status ID | Short Label | Status Group | Triggered By |
| :---- | :---- | :---- | :---- |
| OPEN | Open | OPEN | Auto-set on complaint creation by Employee |
| INPROCESS | In Process | INPROCESS | Manager assigns responsible\_team |
| ON\_HOLD | On Hold | INPROCESS | Responsible Team puts on hold |
| RESOLVED | Resolved | ACCEPTED | Responsible Team / Manager marks resolved |
| CLOSED | Closed | COMPLETED | Manager confirms resolution and closes |
| REOPEN | Re-Opened | OPEN | Employee or Manager reopens a closed complaint |

## **C.3 — Step-by-Step: Adding Status Values**

| \# | Action | Detail |
| ----- | :---- | :---- |
| **1** | Go to Status tab | Select d\_complaints in the tree. Click the Status tab in the Detail panel. |
| **2** | Enable | Switch on the status and click save  and edit  |
| **3** | Add OPEN status | Click the Add Record(+) icon. Fill: Status: OPEN | Description: Open | object\_type: PROCIFY | Status Group : OPEN. Click Save. |
| **4** | Add remaining statuses | Repeat Add for each status: INPROCESS (In Process), ON\_HOLD (On Hold), RESOLVED (Resolved), CLOSED (Closed), REOPEN (Re-Opened) for PROCIFY. Click Save after all are added. |
|  | Repeat for MAVENTIC | Repeat the Action 3 and 4 for object\_type maventic as well. |
| **4** | Verify list | All 12 status values appear in the status list. |

| 💡  Why Status Groups Are Important for This Application |
| :---- |
| Session 7 will use these groups to configure Vienable on buttons and fields. |
| Example: The 'Edit' button will be configured as: Visible only when s\_status\_group \= OPEN\_GROUP. |
| Example: The 'Assign Team' button will be: Visible only when s\_status\_group \= OPEN\_GROUP and Role \= Manager. |
| Without the groups defined now, the Vienable configuration in Session 7 cannot be done. |
|  |

