## Session 4 — Value Helps & Queries {#session-4}

## Session Objectives

By the end of this session, you will be able to:

* Understand the three types of Value Helps (VH Config, VH Query, VH Transaction) and when to use each.

* Create a VH Config for priority (Low / Medium / High / Critical) and nature\_of\_complaint, complaint\_type fields.

* Create VH Query types that fetch data from d\_customers and d\_resp\_team entities as picker suggestions.

* Understand what a KloQuery is and how it differs from a Runtime Entity query.

* Create two Saved Queries for the complaint list: My Complaints (filtered by assigned\_to \= current user) and All Open Complaints (filtered by s\_status \= OPEN).

* Assign Value Helps to entity properties in the BOL Editor.

* Update the UI — wire VH pickers and query-driven TransNodes into the p\_complaints search and detail sections.

| 📘  What This Session Builds On |
| :---- |
| Session 3 configured Relations, Object Types, Status, and ID Series on d\_complaints — the structural foundation. |
| Session 4 makes the user experience intelligent: instead of typing raw IDs, users get dropdowns and pickers. |
| priority, complaint\_type, nature\_of\_complaint → VH Config (fixed, app-managed lists). |
| customer\_id, responsible\_team → VH Query (dynamic, fetched from d\_customers / d\_resp\_team). |
| Saved Queries → drive the different list views per role (My Complaints for Employee, All Open for Manager). |
|  |

## Part A — Understanding Value Helps

## **A.1 — What Is a Value Help?**

A Value Help (VH) is a platform-level component that provides a controlled list of choices for an entity property. When a VH is assigned to a property, the corresponding input field in the UI automatically renders as a dropdown (ComboBox), auto-complete field, or picker dialog (F4) — without any additional coding. The VH also validates that the entered value exists in the list when 'Is Restricted' is enabled.

| 📘  Three VH Types in Klobase (VH User Guide — Section 1.1) |
| :---- |
| VH Config: Values are maintained in the metadata (config) DB. App-managed, flavor-dependent. Can be overridden by child apps. Requires a build after creation/update. Use for fixed classification lists (priority, complaint\_type, etc.). |
| VH Query: Values are fetched from a transactional or modelled query at runtime. Use for dynamic master data pickers (customer\_id, responsible\_team). The query determines what data appears in the picker. |
| VH Transaction (DEPRECATED): Older type. Internally still supported for legacy apps. New development should use VH Config or VH Query instead. |
|  |

| VH Type | Source | Naming | Use Case for This App |
| :---- | :---- | :---- | :---- |
| VH Config | Metadata / Config DB | vhc\_\<name\> | priority, complaint\_type, nature\_of\_complaint |
| VH Query | Transactional / Modelled Query | vhq\_\<name\> | customer\_id → d\_customers, responsible\_team → d\_resp\_team |

## **A.2 — Value Helps to Create in This Session**

| VH ID | Type | Property to Assign | Description |
| :---- | :---- | :---- | :---- |
| vhc\_priority | VH Config | priority | Low / Medium / High / Very High |
| vhc\_complaint\_type | VH Config | complaint\_type | Software | Infra | Hardware |
| vhc\_nature\_of\_complaint | VH Config | nature\_of\_complaint | **Hardware** : Battery not charging | Computer won't power on | Display not working | Keyboard or Mouse malfunction | Overheating and fan noise | Peripheral devices not recognized | Slow performance | Touchpad not responding | Others **Infra**: Firewall blocking access | IP address conflicts | Network latency | Network printer offline | Physical damage to cables | Power outage affecting network | UPS battery failure | Wi-Fi signal drops frequently | Others **Software**: Application crashes on launch | Application hangs or becomes unresponsive | Data loss or corruption | Missing or corrupted files | Software freezes randomly | Syncing issues with cloud services | Unable to install updates | Virus or malware infections | Others |
| vhq\_customer | VH Query | customer\_id | Fetches from d\_customers — key: customer\_id, display: customer\_name |
| vhq\_resp\_team | VH Query | responsible\_team | Fetches from d\_resp\_team — key: emp\_id, display: name |

## Part B — Creating VH Config Value Helps

## **B.1 — What Is VH Config and When to Use It?**

VH Config stores its values in the metadata database — the same database that stores entity definitions, page structures, and app metadata. These values are part of the application definition itself, not the transaction data. Use VH Config for lists that are defined once by the developer and rarely change: priority levels, complaint categories, nature classifications.

## **B.2 — Step-by-Step: Creating vhc\_priority**

Follow these steps to create the priority VH Config. Repeat the process for vhc\_complaint\_type and vhc\_nature.

| \# | Action | Detail |
| :---- | :---- | :---- |
| **1** | **Open Data Studio** | Navigate to Design Studio → Data Studio. Select your application customer\_complaints\_\<prid\> from the dropdown.  |
| **2** | **Click \+ → Value Help** | Select entity. Click the Add icon (+) in the toolbar. A menu appears — select Value Help. The Value Help creation dialog opens. |
| **3** | **Fill VH ID and Type** | Value Help ID: vhc\_priority | Value Help Type: CONFIG. Click Create. The VH appears in the tree on the left. |
| **5** | **Fill VH detail fields** | Description: Priority levels for customer complaints | Key Property: key | Display Text: text | Desc Format: :text (shows only display text in the field, key stored internally). |
| **6** | **Add VH Config items** | Click the Add (+) icon in the VH Config Items section. Add Row 1: Key: LOW | Display Text: Low. Add Row 2: Key: MEDIUM | Display Text: Medium. Add Row 3: Key: HIGH | Display Text: High. Add Row 4: Key: CRITICAL | Display Text: Critical. |
| **7** | **Save** | Click Save. vhc\_priority is saved. Note: a build is required for this to appear in the UI — proceed to create all VH Configs first, then the trainer will trigger the build. |

## **B.3 — Assign VH Config to Entity Properties**

After creating all three VH Configs, assign each to the corresponding property in d\_complaints. This is done in the Properties tab of the entity.

| \# | Action | Detail |
| :---- | :---- | :---- |
| **1** | **Select d\_complaints** | In the Data Studio tree, click d\_complaints. |
| **2** | **Go to Properties tab** | Click the Properties tab. Locate the priority property row. |
| **3** | **Set Value Help for priority** | Click the Value Help column cell for the priority row. A value help picker opens — type vhc\_priority and select it. The VH ID is now assigned to the property. |
| **5** | **Repeat for complaint\_type and nature\_of\_complaint** | Assign vhc\_complaint\_type to complaint\_type property. Assign vhc\_nature to nature\_of\_complaint property. |
| **6** | **Save** | Click Save. All three properties now have VH Config assignments. |

| ✔  VH Config Checkpoint |
| :---- |
| vhc\_priority created — 4 items: LOW, MEDIUM, HIGH, CRITICAL. |
| vhc\_complaint\_type created — 5 items matching the Object Types from Session 3\. |
| vhc\_nature created — 4 items: TECHNICAL, LOGISTICS, COMMERCIAL, GENERAL. |
| All 3 VH Configs assigned to their respective d\_complaints properties. |
| Save confirmed for all. |
