## Part E — Saved Queries

## **E.1 — What Is a Saved Query?**

A Saved Query is a named combination of a base query and a specific set of pre-filled search parameters. Where a Modelled Query defines the structure, a Saved Query defines a pre-configured instance of that query — ready to execute with one click in the search bar. Saved Queries appear as quick-filter options in the search TransNode of the UI.

| 📘  Saved Query vs Modelled Query (Basic Training Guide — Section 2.1) |
| :---- |
| Modelled Query: The definition — entity, properties, parameters, WHERE logic. Reusable structure. |
| Saved Query: A named configuration of a modelled query with preset parameter values. Stored per entity. |
| In the UI, a Saved Query appears as a quick-filter button or dropdown in the search bar section. |
| Saved Queries are created from the Data Browser (Advanced Search), not directly in the query detail tab. |
| Naming convention for Saved Query IDs: Begin with sq\_ by convention. |
|  |

## **E.2 — Step-by-Step: Creating a Saved Query from Data Browser**

| \# | Action | Detail |
| :---- | :---- | :---- |
| **1** | **Open Data Browser** | Select d\_complaints in the Data Studio tree. Click the data browser in the toolbar. The Data Browser opens. |
| **2** | **Open Advanced Search** | In the Data Browser toolbar, click the Advance Search button. A search configuration dialog opens. |
| **3** | **Select Base Query** | In the Query field of the Advance click \+  |
| **4** | **Set parameter values** | Select customer\_id in the left input and like in the middle input. Enter: \$SYSTEM.userID as the parameter value. This means the saved query will always use the logged-in user's ID at runtime. |
| **5** | **Save as Saved Query** | Click the Save Query button (or the Save icon in the Advance Search toolbar). Enter Saved Query ID: sq\_my\_complaints | Description: My Complaints — filter by current user. Click Save. |
| **6** | **Verify** | Click search. The Data Browser should show only complaints where assigned\_to matches the current user (or empty if no matches). The saved query is now available. |

