## Session 5 — BOL Logic & Validations with dashboard creation {#session-5}

By the end of this session, you will be able to:

* Set up the Private Node development environment and understand the TypeScript entity file structure.

* Understand the entity lifecycle and all hook methods: onAfterCreate, OnValidate, onDelete.

* Write and test server-side default value logic: auto-set s\_status \= OPEN and assigned\_to \= current user on complaint creation.

* Implement property-level Getter and Setter overrides to compute derived fields (actual\_tat, full customer display).

* Write business rule validations in OnValidate: mandatory field checks, date constraints, role-based restriction.

* Implement query-level beforeQ / afterQ callbacks: enforce row-level, role-based visibility before a query runs, and reshape/compute values across the result set after it returns.

* Apply the landing page concept: prepare the p\_complaints page to function as the application's primary home screen post-login.

| 📘  What This Session Builds On |
| :---- |
| Sessions 1–4 built the data model, page structure, VH pickers, and queries — all through the low-code tools (Data Studio, Page Builder). |
| Session 5 crosses the threshold into code: you will write TypeScript in the entity .ts file, running server-side in the Klobase Node.js runtime. |
| The entity file is the ONLY place business logic belongs. Never write business rules in the controller file. |
| Session 5 follows the core platform principle: Business logic → Entity file. UI orchestration → Controller file. |
| Reference: PROC-23582 (Validation Logic to Be Maintained in Entity Callback onValidate Methods instead of Controller files). |
|  |

## Part A — Setting Up the Private Node Environment

*Reference: [Private Node setup Guide](https://sites.google.com/procifynow.com/procifyhelpcenter/application-development/reference-manual)*

The Private Node is a local Node.js server running on your laptop that acts as the workspace server for your development session. Instead of your browser talking to the shared landscape server, it talks to your VS Code instance — giving you full debugging capability and instant iteration on server-side code.

### Pre-requisites

| Requirement | How to verify |
| :---- | :---- |
| VS Code installed | Open VS Code — if it opens, you are good. |
| Procify VSIX extension installed | Extensions (Ctrl+Shift+X) → search 'Procifynow'. If not installed, ask the trainer for the VSIX file. |
| Network access to the landscape | Open your landscape URL in a browser. If the login page loads, connectivity is confirmed. |
| Node.js installed | Open a terminal: node \--version. You should see v22+. |

### Setting the Private Node in VS Code {#setting-the-private-node-in-vs-code}

| 1 | Create Your Workspace Folder Create a new folder on your laptop: Documents/PrivateNode/srm\_\<your\_prid\>. All application server-side files will be downloaded here. |
| :---: | :---- |

| 2 | Open the Folder in VS Code File \> Open Folder \> navigate to your new folder \> click Open. |
| :---: | :---- |

| 3 | Open the Command Palette Press Ctrl+Shift+P (Windows/Linux) or Cmd+Shift+P (Mac). |
| :---: | :---- |

| 4 | Run Procify Version Selection Type: Procifynow: Procify IDE Setup and press Enter. A WebView panel opens on the right. |
| :---: | :---- |

| 5 | Enter Your Landscape URL In the WebView, enter your app URL — e.g. https\://dev.procify38.com/p6/srm\_pr0045/index.html. Click Next. |
| :---: | :---- |

| 6 | Enter Developer Credentials Enter your developer username and password. Click Start Setup. |
| :---: | :---- |

| 7 | Wait for Initialisation The VS Code file explorer will populate with folders (entity stubs, controller stubs, kloconfig, etc.). A confirmation message appears when complete. |
| :---: | :---- |

| 8 | Checkout application The VS Code will open a webview to checkout the assigned apps. Select  the created app and perform checkout |
| :---: | :---- |

## **A.2 — Entity File Location and Structure**

The entity .ts file for d\_complaints is located in the Private Node workspace at the path:

| workspace/apps/\<your\_app\_id\>/d\_complaints.ts |
| :---- |

If the file does not exist yet, the private node can generate it. The base structure looks like this:

| import { KloEntity, ValidationError, ErrorType } from 'klobase'; export class d\_complaints extends KloEntity {   // ── Getters & Setters ──────────────────────────────────────────   // ── Lifecycle Hooks ────────────────────────────────────────────   public async onAfterCreate(): Promise\<any\> {     // Called after a new complaint record is created   }   public async OnValidate(): Promise\<any\> {     // Called during navigation if transnode setActiveIf \= Valid   }   public async onDelete(): Promise\<any\> {     // Called after the record is deleted   } } |
| :---- |

| 💡  Architecture Principle: Business Logic Belongs Here |
| :---- |
| The entity file is the authoritative location for all business logic. Period. |
| Controller file → UI orchestration only (navigation, formatter functions, show/hide decisions). |
| Entity file → field defaults, cross-field computations, validations, role enforcement. |
| Never duplicate validation logic in the controller. If the controller calls commitP(), OnValidate() fires automatically on the server — that is your safety net. |
| Reference: kloTouch 7.0 Basic Training Guide — Section 7.5 Best Practice Do's and Don'ts (Entity File). |
|  |

## Part B — Entity Lifecycle Hooks

## **B.1 — The Three Hook Methods**

Klobase provides three entity lifecycle hook methods that you override in the entity .ts file. These run on the server and are triggered automatically by the platform at the appropriate point in the entity's lifecycle.

| Method | When Called | Trigger Condition | Primary Use |
| :---- | :---- | :---- | :---- |
| onAfterCreate() | After entity is created in DB | On every new entity save | Set defaults, auto-populate audit fields, initialise related records |
| OnValidate() | During navigation / on save / validateP function | TransNode setActiveIf \= 'Valid' | Validate field values, enforce business rules, push errors |
| onDelete() | After entity is deleted | On every delete operation | Cascade cleanup, logging, send notifications |

| 📘  OnValidate — Important Details  |
| :---- |
| OnValidate is triggered ONLY when the TransNode's 'setActiveIf' property is set to 'Valid' in the Page Builder. |
| If setActiveIf is not set, OnValidate does NOT fire — the platform skips validation on navigation. |
| Inside OnValidate, push errors using: this.errors.push(new ValidationError(this, propertyId, errorCode, message, ErrorType.ERROR)); |
| The method must NOT return the errors array — it pushes to this.errors and the platform reads it. (PROC-24075 fixed this for proper return type.) |
| ErrorType.ERROR blocks save. ErrorType.WARNING allows save but shows a warning toast. |
| After your OnValidate runs, the platform ALSO runs its own mandatory-field validation based on key attributes — you don't need to repeat those. |
|  |

## **B.2 — Configuring TransNode setActiveIf for Validation**

Before OnValidate can fire, the TransNode that holds the complaint detail must be configured with setActiveIf \= Valid in the Page Builder.

| \# | Action | Detail / Code |
| :---- | :---- | :---- |
| **1** | **Open Page Builder** | Navigate to Design Studio → Page Builder. Select customer\_complaints\_\<prid\>. Click the Edit icon. |
| **2** | **Select the detail TransNode** | Click p\_complaints in the tree. Open the Page Data Model panel. Click on the 'detail' TransNode row. |
| **3** | **Set setActiveIf \= Valid** | In the TransNode detail section (right side), find the 'setActiveIf' property. Change from the default (Always) to 'Valid'. This means: when the user tries to navigate away from the detail form, the platform will call OnValidate first. If errors exist, navigation is blocked. |
| **4** | **Save** | Click Save. |

## Part C — Entity Rules

## **C.1 — What are Entity Rules?**

Rules contain business logic codes. It works with the event bus, any change in the entity will trigger the event, and all the rules hooked with the event will be executed sequentially. Rules are reusable objects across client, server and middleware.

EX: 

1. When a complaint is created  by an employee then status should be OPEN and assigned\_to should be the same user.  
2. A responsible team/manager/assigned\_to can be assigned by the manager only.  
3. An employee or manager can delete the complaint but not the responsible team

| Field | Default Value Logic |
| :---- | :---- |
| s\_status | 'OPEN' — every new complaint starts in OPEN status |
| assigned\_to | this.txn.getUserID() — auto-set to the logged-in user who created the complaint |
| created\_on | this.s\_created\_on — already auto-set by system field, no action needed |
| created\_by | this.s\_created\_by — already auto-set by system field, no action needed |

## **C.2 — How to create an Entity Rule?** {#c.2-—-how-to-create-an-entity-rule?}

**Step 1:** Go to the Rule tab and click on the **add icon(+).**  
**![][image13]**

| Field | Description |
| :---- | :---- |
| Event type  | Select the event type. Based on the type of event you choose, the rule will be executed. |
| Type | Select the channel type, based on this the transaction will be performed. |
| Exit ID | is an ID you put in for the rule subscription. (can select from the existing one). |
| Clazz | This will be the rule file name. |
| Invocation Method | The method needs to be called. |

**Step 2:** Select the Event Type from the drop-down. The event type will mention when the rules will be executed.   
![][image14] 

**Event types:**

* On Create ( rule will be executed on create of an entity)

* On Delete (rule will be executed on delete of an entity)

* On Update (rule will be executed on update of an entity)

* Rest of the types will be covered in future.

 **Step 3:** Select the channel type(there are different input channels to perform a transaction on an entity, channel type selection decides when to trigger the Rule).

**Channel Type:**

* Endpoint / Device on Klarion server- always on the client

* Endpoint / Device on Klarion server

* **Endpoint / Device on Klarion server- always on server(current focus)**

* Endpoint / Device to Server/ Client \-Network Context Switch

* Legacy on Klarion server

* Klarion server to Klarion offline server

* Klarion server to Klarion Server

![][image15]

**Step 4:** Fill in the value for **Exit ID.**

**Exit ID:** It's an ID that you put in for the rule subscription. (can select from the existing one).

![][image16]

**Step 5:** In the Clazz field provide the rule file name (example: rules/\<file\_name\>) and in the invocation method provide an event name. You will be using that event to write your business logic codes.

* **NOTE:** If you are creating a **Rule** for the same entity you can fill **this** inside the **Clazz** field.

![][image17]

**Step 6:** Click on the **save** button to save the newly created rule.  
![][image18]

## **C.3 — Implementing onCreateRule for default values**

As mentioned above, create a rule for onCreate with channel type as ALWAYS\_ON\_SERVER with class as this.

Create Entity Folder and d\_complaints.ts file 

| public async onCreateComplaint(oEvent:any): Promise\<any\> {   // Auto-set status to OPEN on every new complaint   let complaint \= oEvent.getObject();   complaint.s\_status \= 'OPEN';   // Auto-set assigned\_to to the creating user's login ID   const currentUser \= this.txn.getUserID();   complaint.assigned\_to \= currentUser;   // Log for debugging (visible in Private Node terminal)   this.info('Complaint created by:', currentUser, '| Status set to OPEN'); } |
| :---- |

| 📘  this.txn — The Transaction Object (BOL API Reference) |
| :---- |
| Inside entity hooks, oEvent.getTxn() gives you access to the KloTransaction object — the server-side context for the current request. |
| oEvent.getTxn().getUserID() → returns the login ID of the user who triggered the save. Use for audit fields. |
|  |
| oEvent.getTxn().getValueBag() → a key-value store local to the current transaction. Good for passing data between hooks. |
| this.info(), this.warn(), this.error() → server-side logging methods. Output appears in the Private Node terminal and the Google Cloud logs. |
|  |

