# **Part A — Menu Editor**

## **A.1 — What Is the Menu Editor?**

The Menu Editor (also called Menu Builder per PROC-26419) is the tool you use to define the navigation structure visible to users in the hamburger menu of the application at runtime. Without a menu entry, users cannot navigate between screens — they would need to know URLs manually.

Access path: Running app → Hamburger menu (☰) → Dev Tools → Menu Editor.

## **A.2 — Menu Fields Reference**

| Field | Description | Rules / Notes |
| ----- | ----- | ----- |
| Menu ID | Unique identifier for this menu item. | ONLY alphanumeric \+ underscores. NO hyphens ( \- ). NO spaces. Case-sensitive. Wrong: mn-complaints. Correct: mn\_complaints. |
| Target Page | Page ID the menu item navigates to. | Use the Value Help (VH) icon to pick from existing pages. Do NOT type the page ID manually. |
| Menu Text | Label shown to the user in the hamburger menu. | Keep it short. Title case. Localisation supported. |
| Page Parameters | Optional JSON for navTo params (SS, QP, D, Mode). | Leave blank for simple page nav. Not needed for the Complaints entry. |
| Menu Icon | SAP icon URI displayed alongside the text. | Format: sap-icon://\<icon-name\>. Must be a valid SAP UI5 icon name. Example: sap-icon://customer-and-contacts. |
| Sequence | Sort order in the menu. Lower number \= appears first. | Use multiples of 10 (10, 20, 30\) to leave room for future items. |
| Roles | Restrict visibility to specific application roles. | Leave BLANK \= visible to ALL roles. Fill role IDs (comma-separated) to restrict. |
| Test Navigation | Validates the menu item navigates correctly. | Always click Test Navigation before saving — catches target page errors early. |

## **A.3 — BRD Menu Structure to Implement**

| Menu Field | Value |
| ----- | ----- |
| Menu ID | mn\_complaints |
| Target Page | p\_complaints (select from VH) |
| Menu Text | Complaints |
| Menu Icon | sap-icon://customer-and-contacts |
| Sequence | 10 |
| Roles | Leave blank — visible to ALL roles (Employee, Manager, IT Team) |
| Page Parameters | Not required — leave blank |

## **A.4 — Step-by-Step: Creating the Application Menu**

| 1 | Open Menu Editor In the running application, open the hamburger menu (☰ top-left). Go to Dev Tools → Menu Editor. |
| :---: | :---- |
| **2** | Select the Application In the App dropdown at the top of the Menu Editor, select customer\_complaints\_\<prid\>. Click the Edit button (pencil icon ✏). The menu table becomes editable. |
| **3** | Add a Header Group Click Add (+) → Add Header. Fill in:    Menu ID: hdr\_main    Menu Text: Main    Sequence: 10    Roles: leave blank Click OK. (A Header groups child items visually in the menu.) |
| **4** | Add the Complaints Child Menu Item Select the hdr\_main row in the table. Click Add (+) → Add Child. Fill in:    Menu ID: mn\_complaints    Target Page: p\_complaints (use VH picker)    Menu Text: Complaints    Menu Icon: sap-icon://customer-and-contacts    Sequence: 10    Roles: leave blank Click OK. |
| **5** | Test the Navigation Click Test Navigation on the mn\_complaints row. p\_complaints should open correctly. If a 'Page not found' error appears — check that the Target Page is exactly p\_complaints (no typos). |
| **6** | Save Click the Save button (disk icon 💾). Wait for the busy dialog to close. A success toast confirms the menu is saved. |
| **7** | Verify with All Personas Log out and log in separately as emp\_\<prid\>, mgr\_\<prid\>, and it\_\<prid\>. In each session, open the hamburger menu. The 'Complaints' item must be visible and navigate to p\_complaints for ALL three test users. |

| ⚠  Important Common Menu Editor Mistakes Hyphen in Menu ID: mn-complaints causes the item to save but NEVER appear in the app. Always use underscore: mn\_complaints. Typing Target Page manually: Always use the VH picker. Manual typos cause silent navigation failures. Saving without testing: Always click 'Test Navigation' before saving. It catches missing pages early. Role restriction accident: If the menu item is set to a specific role, other personas cannot see it. Leave Roles blank for all-role visibility. |
| :---- |

# **Part B — HomePage Editor**

## **B.1 — What Is the HomePage Editor?**

The HomePage Editor configures which screen tiles appear on the application's built-in Home grid — accessible via the Home (🏠) icon. This is separate from the Landing Page (p\_complaint\_dashboard configured in Session 5). The Landing Page loads automatically on login. The Home grid gives users tile-based navigation to frequently used screens.

| 📘  Reference / Tip Three 'home' concepts — don't confuse them Landing Page (Session 5): Configured via Landing Page Settings. Auto-loads after login. Shows p\_complaint\_dashboard. HomePage Editor: Tile grid shown when user clicks the Home (🏠) icon. Configure p\_complaints and p\_complaint\_dashboard tiles here. p\_home\_page (BRD): A screen defined in the BRD. Can be built as an additional home screen — not required for this session's delivery. |
| :---- |

## **B.2 — HomePage Editor Fields Reference**

| Field | Description |
| ----- | ----- |
| HomePageID | Unique ID for this tile. Convention: hp\_\<screen\_short\_name\>. |
| Screen | The Page ID this tile navigates to. Use VH to select. |
| Position | Where the tile appears on the home grid. Use 'Body' for standard tiles. |
| Sequence | Order of the tile. Lower \= appears first. |
| Role | Restrict tile visibility to specific roles. Leave blank \= all roles. |

## **B.3 — Step-by-Step: Adding Tiles to HomePage Editor**

| 1 | Open HomePage Editor Hamburger menu (☰) → Dev Tools → HomePage Editor. Select customer\_complaints\_\<prid\> from the App dropdown. Click the Edit icon (✏). |
| :---: | :---- |
| **2** | Add the Dashboard Tile Click Add (+). HomePageID: hp\_dashboard Screen: p\_complaint\_dashboard (select via VH) Position: Body Sequence: 5 Role: leave blank (all roles) Click Save (do NOT navigate away yet). |
| **3** | Add the Complaints Tile Click Add (+). HomePageID: hp\_all\_complaints Screen: p\_complaints (select via VH) Position: Body Sequence: 10 Role: leave blank (all roles) Click Save. |
| **4** | Save All Click the Save icon (💾) in the toolbar. Wait for busy dialog to close. Success toast confirms. |
| **5** | Verify Log out. Log in as emp\_\<prid\>. Click the Home (🏠) icon. Two tiles must appear: 'Dashboard' and 'Complaints'. Click each tile — verify it navigates to the correct screen. Repeat with mgr\_\<prid\> and it\_\<prid\>. |

# **Part C — App Status Lifecycle & Versioning**

## **C.1 — App Status Values**

Every version of an application on the Procify platform has a Status that controls what operations are allowed on it. Understanding these statuses is essential before releasing or transporting.

| Status | Meaning | Can Develop? | Can Transport? | Can Create New Version? |
| ----- | ----- | ----- | ----- | ----- |
| ACTIVE | Current working version. All development happens here. | ✅ Yes | ❌ No | ❌ No |
| RELEASED | Development complete and locked. Ready for transport. | ❌ No | ✅ Yes | ✅ Yes (create from RELEASED) |
| DEPRECATED | Retired. Superseded by a newer version. | ❌ No | ❌ No | ❌ No |
| DISCARDED | Abandoned branch/version. Excluded from main path. | ❌ No | ❌ No | ❌ No |

| ⚠  Important Critical Versioning Rules — memorise these before proceeding A NEW VERSION can only be created from a RELEASED version. A BRANCH can only be created from an ACTIVE version. Branch flavors CANNOT be transported. Merge the branch into a new version first, then transport. Once status is changed to RELEASED, no further development is possible on that version. Always build (Validate & Publish) BEFORE changing status to RELEASED. |
| :---- |

## **C.2 — Step-by-Step: Releasing the Application**

| 1 | Navigate to Account Console (also called Workspace Hub) Open the hamburger menu → Sys Admin → Account Console (or Workspace Hub). Alternatively: Hamburger menu → My Apps (if visible in your role). |
| :---: | :---- |
| **2** | Find Your Application In the Apps list, search for customer\_complaints\_\<prid\>. Click the App ID to open the application detail screen. |
| **3** | Open Edit Mode Click the Edit button (pencil icon ✏) in the toolbar. The Status field becomes editable. |
| **4** | Change Status to RELEASED In the Status dropdown, select RELEASED. Read the confirmation message carefully — this action locks all development on this version. Click Save. The app status changes to RELEASED. The Edit icon in Data Studio and Page Builder will now be disabled for this version. |
| **5** | Verify In the App detail, the Status badge shows RELEASED. Open Data Studio → select customer\_complaints\_\<prid\> → click the Edit icon. You should see a message that the app is locked for editing. |

# **Part D — App Branching and Merging**

## **D.1 — Why Branch?**

A Branch creates a parallel ACTIVE copy of your application. Use it when you need to work on next-phase enhancements while the current version is being tested in QA. The branch is fully isolated — changes in the branch don't affect the main RELEASED version until you explicitly merge them.

| Scenario | Use Branch? |
| ----- | ----- |
| Bug fix on the current version in QA | ❌ No — fix directly in main, create new minor version |
| New feature development while current is in QA | ✅ Yes — branch from current ACTIVE version |
| Experimental UI changes for client review | ✅ Yes — branch lets you show without committing to main |

## **D.2 — Step-by-Step: Creating a Branch**

| 1 | Go to Account Console → My Apps Locate customer\_complaints\_\<prid\>. The app must be in ACTIVE status to create a branch. |
| :---: | :---- |
| **2** | Click Branch In the app detail view, click the Branch button (visible only when status \= ACTIVE). The platform creates a new Branch Flavor automatically. The branch version appears in the My Apps list with a branch indicator. |
| **3** | Assign Users to the Branch Click the branch version to open its detail. Go to the Roles & Users tab → click Edit. Assign ALL three test users and your developer account to their respective roles. ⚠ Users are NOT copied automatically from the parent — you must assign them manually in the branch. |
| **4** | Develop in the Branch Open Data Studio or Page Builder. In the App dropdown, select the BRANCH version (not the main version). Make your changes. Save and test independently. |

## **D.3 — Branch Merge Workflow (Summary)**

When the branch is ready to be merged back into the main release cycle:

| 1 | Export Both ZIPs Account Console → Export section. Download ZIP of the main RELEASED version (Set 1\) and the branch version (Set 2). |
| :---: | :---- |
| **2** | Open App Compare & Merge Account Console → App Compare & Merge. Upload Set 1 (main) as Left and Set 2 (branch) as Right. Click Compare. |
| **3** | Review the Differences The tool lists differences per component: entities, pages, queries, VH, menus, etc. For each difference, select which version to keep: Set 1 (main), Set 2 (branch), or a Custom combination. |
| **4** | Download Merged ZIP Once all decisions are made, click Download to export the merged ZIP. |
| **5** | Create New Version and Import In Account Console, create a new Major or Minor version from the current RELEASED main version. Import the merged ZIP into the new version. Run Update Schema, Full Build, and test before releasing. |

| 📘  Reference / Tip Reference: App Branching & Merging User Guide — App\_Branching\_\_\_Merging-User\_Guide.docx PROC-26419 note: 'Account Console' may be renamed 'Workspace Hub' in platform help content. Both names refer to the same tool. |
| :---- |

# **Part E — Validate & Publish**

## **E.1 — Validate vs. Publish — What's the Difference?**

| Action | What It Does | When to Use |
| ----- | ----- | ----- |
| Validate | Scans the application for errors WITHOUT building. Safe — nothing changes in the running app. | Run first, every time. Fix all errors before triggering Publish. |
| Publish | Validates first, then builds the app if no blocking errors exist. Updates the running app. | After Validate passes with 0 errors. Use Full Build for first publish or after VH Config changes. |

## **E.2 — Common Publish Blockers**

| Blocker | Cause | Fix |
| ----- | ----- | ----- |
| VH Config not visible in UI after Publish | VH Config changes require a Full Build. | Always select 'Full Build' for the first publish or after adding/editing VH Configs. |
| TypeScript compilation error | Syntax error in entity .ts or controller .ts file. | Open VS Code → check terminal for tsc errors → fix, save, re-validate. |
| Update Schema not run | Entity properties added but schema not updated in DB. | Data Studio → select entity → Update Schema → confirm success toast. |
| Another build IN-QUEUE | A previous build is already running. | Click 'Proceed' to queue behind the current build. Do NOT cancel it. |
| Missing app\_util flavor | Validate and Publish menu not visible. | Ask trainer — app\_util must be assigned to your developer account. |

## **E.3 — Step-by-Step: Validate and Publish**

| 1 | Open Validate and Publish Hamburger menu (☰) → Dev Tools → Validate and Publish. (If this menu item is missing, app\_util flavor is not assigned to your account. Ask the trainer.) |
| :---: | :---- |
| **2** | Enter the Application In the App field, type customer\_complaints\_\<prid\> and press Enter. The application version details load. |
| **3** | Run Validate First Select Action: Validate. Click Submit. Wait for the validation report to generate. Download the XLSX report if errors are listed. Fix every ERROR before proceeding. Warnings can be reviewed but do not block the build. |
| **4** | Fix All Errors For each error in the report:   \- Missing Update Schema → Data Studio → Update Schema on the entity.   \- TypeScript error → VS Code → fix tsc error → save → re-validate.   \- Missing VH Build → select Full Build in the next step. Re-run Validate until the report shows 0 errors. |
| **5** | Run Publish Select Action: Publish. Build Type: Full Build (required for first publish and after any VH Config changes). Click Submit. The Publish Log shows the progression:    IN-QUEUE → IN-PROCESS → PUBLISHED Do NOT navigate away while the build is running. |
| **6** | Verify the Published App Log out completely. Log back in. Open the hamburger menu → confirm 'Complaints' item is visible. Navigate to p\_complaints → confirm live data loads. Verify: status badges render, VH dropdowns populate, all tabs in detail section work. |

| 📘  Reference / Tip Publish Logs reference: Publish\_Logs\_Screen.docx — explains each status stage and error codes. Validate & Publish reference: Publish\_or\_Validate\_Screen-7\_0\_App\_utility\_\_.docx |
| :---- |

# **Part F — Transport: DEV to QA**

## **F.1 — How Transport Works**

Transport is a PULL mechanism: the TARGET system (QA) asks the SOURCE system (DEV) for the latest published version. You perform ALL transport steps while logged into the QA environment, not DEV.

| Stage | What Happens |
| ----- | ----- |
| Setup | Transport creates entries in QA metadata tables for the application, groups, and flavors. |
| Download | Application ZIPs are pulled from DEV and extracted in the QA server's file system. |
| Update Schema | Database tables for all entities are created or altered in the QA database. |
| Finalising | Application is set to ACTIVE in QA so users can log in and access it. |

| ⚠  Important Transport Pre-requisites — verify ALL before starting 1\. The application must be in RELEASED status on the DEV system. 2\. Branch flavors CANNOT be transported. Create a new version, merge, then transport. 3\. The QA server's config.json must have mm\_klo\_online\_server pointing to the DEV landscape URL. 4\. A host file entry for the DEV system must exist on the QA server. 5\. The transport\_1 app must be assigned to the developer account on the QA system. If any pre-requisite is missing, the transport will fail at Setup or Download stage. Ask the trainer to verify the QA environment config before proceeding. |
| :---- |

## **F.2 — Step-by-Step: Transporting to QA**

| 1 | Log in to the QA System Open the QA environment URL in a browser (trainer provides the URL). Log in with your developer credentials. ⚠ ALL remaining steps are done on QA, not on DEV. |
| :---: | :---- |
| **2** | Navigate to App Transport Hamburger menu (☰) → Sys Admin → App Transport. The App Transport screen lists all available applications from the connected DEV system. |
| **3** | Find Your Application In the list, locate customer\_complaints\_\<prid\>. If not visible, toggle the 'Show All' button to see all available apps (not just those with pending updates). |
| **4** | Click the App Row Click on customer\_complaints\_\<prid\>. A detail panel opens showing available versions from DEV and any already-installed versions in QA. |
| **5** | Install As Writable Click the available version update. A dialog appears with two options:    As Readable — locked, cannot edit in QA.    As Writable — editable, allows continued development in QA. Click 'As Writable' (recommended for training so you can fix issues in QA if needed). |
| **6** | Accept Conditions and Proceed A mandatory conditions dialog appears. For each mandatory item (⚠ icon), tick the acceptance checkbox. If prompted about shared flavor movement (on QA/PROD systems), click Proceed. Click Proceed to start the transport. |
| **7** | Monitor Transport Stages The screen shows progress through: Setup → Download → Update Schema → Finalising. Wait for each stage to complete. The overall status changes to ACTIVE when done. |
| **8** | Assign Users in QA After transport, the app is ACTIVE in QA but has NO users assigned. Go to Account Console → My Apps → customer\_complaints\_\<prid\>. Go to Roles & Users tab → assign:    emp\_\<prid\> → Employee role    mgr\_\<prid\> → Manager role    it\_\<prid\> → ResponsibleTeam role    Your developer account → Manager role |
| **9** | Verify in QA Log out of the QA developer session. Log in as emp\_\<prid\> in QA. Verify: hamburger menu shows 'Complaints', p\_complaints loads, all VH dropdowns work. Repeat with mgr\_\<prid\> and it\_\<prid\>. |

| 📘  Reference / Tip Transport User Guide: Transport-\_User\_Guide.docx The Transport tool supports DEV → QA, QA → PROD, and DEV → DEV movement. Script Master (second tab in Transport screen) shows automated scripts maintained for the system — ask trainer if any scripts need to be run after transport. |
| :---- |

# **Part G — End-to-End Smoke Test**

This smoke test must be completed AFTER publish. It verifies every major feature built across all 8 sessions works correctly in the published application. Run it on DEV first, then repeat on QA after transport.

| \# | Persona | Action | Expected Result |
| ----- | ----- | ----- | ----- |
| 1 | EMPLOYEE (emp\_\<prid\>) | Login → Menu → Complaints. Click Add (with Object Type \= PROCIFY). Fill complaint\_desc (10+ chars), priority \= HIGH, select customer from picker. Click Save. | complaint\_id \= P-000XX auto-generated. s\_status \= OPEN. assigned\_to \= emp\_\<prid\>. Record appears in list. |
| 2 | EMPLOYEE | Open the new complaint → Edit → Clear complaint\_desc → Save. | V001 error blocks save: 'Complaint description must be at least 10 characters'. |
| 3 | EMPLOYEE | Edit → priority dropdown. | ComboBox shows Low / Medium / High / Critical (from vhc\_priority). |
| 4 | EMPLOYEE | Edit → customer\_id field. | F4 picker opens showing d\_customers records. |
| 5 | MANAGER (mgr\_\<prid\>) | Login → open the OPEN complaint → Edit → set responsible\_team from picker → Save. | responsible\_team field is editable for Manager. Record saves. Status remains OPEN. |
| 6 | IT TEAM (it\_\<prid\>) | Login → open assigned complaint → Edit → s\_status \= INPROCESS → Save. | Status badge changes to INPROCESS (orange colour). Edit button remains visible. |
| 7 | EMPLOYEE | Open INPROCESS complaint → Edit → try s\_status \= CLOSED → Save. | V005 error: 'Employees cannot close a complaint'. |
| 8 | MANAGER | Open INPROCESS complaint → Edit → s\_status \= CLOSED → closed\_on \= today → Save. | Status badge changes to CLOSED (grey). Edit button disappears (COMPLETED Vienable). actual\_tat computed in Data Browser. |
| 9 | ALL ROLES | Navigate to Home (🏠). | Two tiles visible: Dashboard and Complaints. Each tile navigates correctly. |
| 10 | ALL ROLES | Preview In → Phone (Page Builder). | Grid in s\_complaints shows only 3 columns (ticket\_id, priority, s\_status) on phone view. |

# **Concepts Recap — Session 8**

| Concept | What It Does | Where to Configure |
| ----- | ----- | ----- |
| Menu Editor (Menu Builder) | Defines hamburger menu structure. Roles blank \= all users. NO hyphens in Menu ID. | Dev Tools → Menu Editor → select app → Edit |
| Header \+ Child Item | Header groups child menu items visually. Children point to actual screens. | Menu Editor → Add → Add Header, then Add → Add Child |
| sap-icon:// | SAP UI5 icon URI format used in menu and controls. | Menu Editor → Menu Icon field |
| HomePage Editor | Adds tiles to the Home (🏠) grid. Separate from Landing Page. | Dev Tools → HomePage Editor |
| Landing Page Settings | Sets auto-load screen after login. Configured in Session 5\. | Dev Tools → Landing Page Settings |
| App Status: RELEASED | Locks development. Enables transport. New version must be created from RELEASED. | Account Console → My Apps → Status field |
| Branch | Parallel ACTIVE copy for next-phase development. Not transportable directly. | Account Console → My Apps → Branch button (ACTIVE only) |
| App Compare & Merge | Compares two ZIP exports; per-component merge decisions; produces merged ZIP. | Account Console → App Compare & Merge |
| Validate | Checks for errors. Safe — nothing deployed. Download XLSX report. | Dev Tools → Validate and Publish → Action: Validate |
| Publish (Full Build) | Validates then builds. VH Config changes REQUIRE Full Build. | Dev Tools → Validate and Publish → Action: Publish → Full Build |
| Transport (Pull) | QA pulls RELEASED app from DEV. Requires config, host, transport\_1 app. | QA system → Sys Admin → App Transport |

# **Session 8 Assignment — Final Delivery**

| 🎓  This Is the Final Session of the Beginner Training Track The Customer Complaints Application is now a fully published, transportable product. All three personas (Employee, Manager, IT Team) have correct access, menu, and data visibility. After this assignment you will have built and shipped a complete enterprise application from scratch. |
| :---- |

## **Mandatory Tasks**

| \# | Task | Verify By |
| ----- | ----- | ----- |
| 1 | Configure the Menu Editor with hdr\_main header and mn\_complaints child item pointing to p\_complaints with icon sap-icon://customer-and-contacts. | All 3 test users see 'Complaints' in hamburger menu after login. |
| 2 | Add HomePage tiles: hp\_dashboard (Sequence 5\) and hp\_all\_complaints (Sequence 10\) for all roles. | Home (🏠) icon shows both tiles and each navigates correctly. |
| 3 | Run Validate → fix ALL errors → re-run until 0 errors. | Validation report shows 0 errors. |
| 4 | Publish (Full Build) → wait for PUBLISHED status. | Logout and login — app loads correctly with all features working. |
| 5 | Change app status to RELEASED in Account Console. | Status badge shows RELEASED. Edit icon is disabled. |
| 6 | Create a Branch from the ACTIVE version (if instructed). Assign all 3 test users to the branch. | Branch appears in My Apps list. Users can login to branch version. |
| 7 | Transport to QA (trainer-guided). Assign users in QA. | App is ACTIVE in QA. All test users can login. p\_complaints loads end-to-end. |
| 8 | Run the full 10-step smoke test above on DEV and on QA. | All 10 smoke test checks pass on both environments. |

